.TH CSTRING 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CSTRING \- overview of the cstring dynamic string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A cstr is a string on the heap that grows when an operation needs more
space. Its buffer always holds a NUL-terminated C string, so you can give
the pointer from cstr_c_str(3) directly to any function that takes a
const char *. A cstr holds text: its content stops at the first NUL
byte.

The capacity of the buffer is always a power of two, and it is never less
than 16 bytes. When an operation needs more space, it grows the buffer
to the smallest power of two that holds the result. cstr_reserve(3)
grows the buffer before you need the space, and cstr_reset(3) makes the
buffer smaller again, to 16 bytes.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, for example cstr_construct(3),
    cstr_append(3) and cstr_replace(3). When an operation fails, these
    macros stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
  - The raw functions, for example cstring_create_full(3),
    cstring_append(3) and cstring_replace(3), which report a failure in
    their return value.
cstr_copy(3) and cstr_split(3) differ from the other macros: they call
their functions directly, and they give NULL on failure.

Each argument that gives text to add is a plain C string. The calls that
change a string (cstr_append(3), cstr_prepend(3), cstr_insert(3),
cstr_set(3) and their cstring_ functions) accept a str argument that
points into the buffer of that same string, as in
cstr_append(s, cstr_c_str(s)). The call reads str correctly even when
it reallocates the buffer.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
The pointer from cstr_c_str(3) points into the buffer of the string.
The string owns the buffer, and the caller never frees it. Each operation
that changes the string can move the buffer, so the pointer is valid
only until the next such operation.

cstr_substring(3), cstr_copy(3) and cstr_split(3) give new strings,
which the caller owns and must destroy. cstr_split(3) gives a cvec of
cstr: the caller destroys each element, and then the vector.
.fi
.SH "USE WITH THE MAPS"
.nf
chashmap and cbstmap do not accept cstr as a key type or a value type;
they accept char *, and they hash and compare such a key by its content.
Give the result of cstr_c_str(3), cast to char *, as the key or the
value. The map copies the characters into its own storage during the
insert, so after the insert you can change or destroy the cstr without
any effect on the map.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A string has no internal lock. A caller can share one string between
threads, but must then serialize each call that changes the string
against all the other calls on that string.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

int main(void) {
    cstr_construct(path, "/var/log");
    cstr_append(path, "/");
    cstr_append(path, "app.log");
    printf("%s (%zu)\en", cstr_c_str(path), cstr_length(path));
    cstr_destroy(path);
    return 0;
}
.fi
